  "findings": [
    "The current programme and adopted appendix identify the English track, 180 EC and the two majors. Culture is first in the programme and major table and directly continues the international-comparative identity. Retained it independently of UCR fit. The Dutch Urban Issues track is not substituted. Current public and formal credit allocations agree; zero-credit GROW remains a non-credit development trajectory.",
    "Twelve first-year units total 60 with their actual 3/5/6/8 credit values. Year 2 has 36 common EC, including the Culture research-project variant 424017-B-12, plus four 6 EC Culture major units. Year 3 non-exchange has 12 EC placement, 18 open, 12 restricted, 12 thesis and 6 Social Philosophy. The I-43 footnote permits two courses from the non-selected major. Instantiated Globalization and Social Risks and Global Migration, the first two in the current public list, each 6 EC, rather than leaving a restricted block unexplained. The 18 EC may be minor/free-elective combinations and remain generic.",
    "Placement course 421098-B-12 requires at least 336 hours in a relevant organisation, an approved learning plan and signed contract before starting, organisation supervision in coordinator consultation and a reflective pass/fail report. Full time or part time across a semester/year is permitted. No host is assumed. Globalisation and Migration run in Blocks 1/2; a coherent final year puts these 12 EC with placement and six open EC in Autumn, then the normal Spring thesis, Social Philosophy and twelve open EC. No named minor timetable is asserted.",
    "The Culture thesis is 400993-B-12 and must be written in English. It is individually empirical, usually using secondary quantitative or qualitative data within available supervisory expertise, with proposal, article-style paper, peer feedback and symposium. The adopted I-43 footnote requires all first-year subjects AND 60 postpropaedeutic EC; completing both earlier years meets it. This controls the catalogue’s less specific 120-credit summary. Autumn thesis entry is exceptional and not used.",
    "Skills 1 develops academic reading/searching. Skills 2 recommends 1 and develops empirical-paper writing; Skills 3 recommends both earlier skills plus introductory methods with statistics simultaneously. Year-1 Project 1 applies these foundations. Culture Project 2 uses prior MTO quantitative/qualitative knowledge, and begins in Spring alongside questionnaire methods. This preparation is recorded without inventing a conflicting requirement that the whole concurrent course finish first.",
    "Actual Culture content: classics and cultural reproduction, individualisation and secularisation; identities constructed through class, gender, ethnicity and nation in relations of power; climate denial/action/adaptation narratives and social structure; and European family sociology linking macro change, gender, intergenerational disadvantage and family behaviour. The full first-year theory/method sequence prepares this material. Questionnaire methods explicitly includes validity, PCA, factor analysis and reliability, while causal methods covers ANOVA, correlations, linear/logistic/path modelling. Social Philosophy treats power, group agency/responsibility, disagreement and epistemic injustice. These contents govern the bounded alignments, not course titles alone."
  ]
